{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775347916-cB2yLmnaAXJ0a6JBhVKT","role":"assistant","created":1775347937,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Looking at the Aug 1939 table:\n- Row 5: `<tr><td>Secret Service... internal Revenue...</td><td>990</td><td>284</td><td>139</td><td>1,135</td><td>+145</td></tr>`\n  - This is weird - \"Secret Service... internal Revenue...\" but only one set of numbers\n  - 139 cases closed - this seems to be for both Secret Service AND Internal Revenue? Or is this just Secret Service?\n\nWait, the next row is:\n- Row 6: `<tr><td></td><td>396</td><td>20</td><td>11</td><td>405</td><td>+9</td></tr>`\n\nSo actually, this second row (with empty first cell) seems to be the Internal Revenue row! That would mean:\n- Internal Revenue cases closed for Aug 1939 = 11\n\nWait, but the text says \"Secret Service... internal Revenue...\" on the same row as 139. This is confusing.\n\nActually, looking at it more carefully - the row 5 with \"Secret Service... internal Revenue...\" seems to be a label that spans both Secret Service and Internal Revenue, but the data in that row (139) might be for Secret Service, with the second row (11) being for Internal Revenue.\n\nHmm, but actually looking at the raw text I captured earlier from the files, let me re-examine the data I saw:\n\nFrom the output earlier, I saw:\n- For Aug 1939: `<tr><td>Secret Service... internal Revenue...</td><td>990</td><td>284</td><td>139</td><td>1,135</td><td>+145</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>396</td><td>20</td><td>11</td><td>405</td><td>+9</td></tr>`\n\nThis is quite confusing. \n\nI think the first row represents Secret Service with 139 cases closed, and the second row with the empty cell is for Internal Revenue with 11 cases closed. Looking at the pattern from Nov 1938's data where the table structure is clear - \"Internal Revenue\" appears in the first cell with cases closed being the 4th cell in each row. The empty first cell in row 6 suggests this is where Internal Revenue data belongs.\n\nChecking against July's data (19 cases closed) and September's (5 cases closed), the 11 cases for August seems consistent with the monthly variations.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_5fe9e46a5aa7465abdc983b3","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\nimport re\n\n# Let me check all the files with a more robust approach\n# Looking at the raw text I captured earlier for each month:\n\n# Nov 1938 (from file): \"Internal Revenue</td><td>388</td><td>4</td><td>20</td><td>372</td><td>-16\"\n# -> 20 cases closed ✓\n\n# Dec 1938 (from file): \"Service.. Internal Revenue</td><td>968 372</td><td>347 12</td><td>343 5</td>\"\n# -> This is a COMBINED row with Secret Service and Internal Revenue\n# -> \"343 5\" means 343 for Secret Service and 5 for Internal Revenue\n# -> 5 cases closed ✓\n\n# Jan 1939 (from file): \"Internal Revenue..</td><td>379</td><td>4</td><td>12</td><td>371</td><td>-8\"\n# -> 12 cases closed ✓\n\n# Feb 1939 (from file): \"Internal Revenue...</td><td>371</td><td>61</td><td>13</td><td>419</td><td>+48\"\n# -> 13 cases closed ✓\n\n# Mar 1939 (from file): \"Internal Revenue...</td><td>419</td><td>13</td><td>11</td><td>421</td><td>+2\"\n# -> 11 cases closed ✓\n\n# Apr 1939 (from file): \"Internal Revenue...</td><td>421</td><td>9</td><td>16</td><td>414</td><td>-7\"\n# -> 16 cases closed ✓\n\n# May 1939 (from file): \"Internal Revenue</td><td>414</td><td>6</td><td>12</td><td>408</td><td>-6\"\n# -> 12 cases closed ✓\n\n# Jun 1939 (from file): \"Internal Revenue...</td><td>408</td><td>14</td><td>27</td><td>395</td><td>-13\"\n# -> 27 cases closed ✓\n\n# Jul 1939 (from file): \"Internal Revenue</td><td>395</td><td>20</td><td>19</td><td>396</td><td>+1\"\n# -> 19 cases closed ✓\n\n# Aug 1939 - need to verify more carefully\nwith open(\"/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1939_10_page_68.txt\", 'r') as f:\n    content = f.read()\n\n# Find the row with \"Secret Service... internal Revenue...\"\n# There seems to be TWO rows - one with label and one with empty first cell\n# Let's parse both to see what's happening\n\n# Looking at the raw output from my earlier grep:\n# <tr><td>Secret Service... internal Revenue...</td><td>990</td><td>284</td><td>139</td>\n# <tr><td></td><td>396</td><td>20</td><td>11</td>\n\n# I think the 139 is for Secret Service and 11 is for Internal Revenue\n# But wait, this could also mean 139 includes both...\n\n# Let me check another month to understand the pattern\n# Looking at Jul 1939 from the raw output:\n# <tr><td>Internal Revenue</td><td>395</td><td>20</td><td>19</td><td>396</td><td>+1</td></tr>\n\n# For Aug 1939, we have TWO rows. The pattern seems to be:\n# Row 1: Secret Service... internal Revenue... with data 990, 284, 139\n# Row 2: empty cell with data 396, 20, 11\n\n# Given the label \"internal Revenue\" is on the first row, and the empty row is below,\n# I believe the empty row (with 11) is actually the Internal Revenue data\n\n# So Aug 1939: 11 cases closed\n\n# Sep 1939 (from file): \"Revenue....</td><td>405</td><td>52</td><td>5</td><td>452</td><td>+ 47\"\n# -> 5 cases closed ✓\n\n# Oct 1939 (from file): \"Internal Revenue...</td><td>452</td><td>33</td><td>31</td><td>454</td><td>+2\"\n# -> 31 cases closed ✓\n\n# Now let me verify by searching in each file more carefully\nfor month, filepath in [\n    (\"Nov 1938\", \"/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1939_01_page_81.txt\"),\n    (\"Dec 1938\", \"/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1939_02_page_111.txt\"),\n    (\"Jan 1939\", \"/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1939_03_page_117.txt\"),\n    (\"Feb 1939\", \"/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1939_04_page_95.txt\"),\n    (\"Mar 1939\", \"/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1939_05_page_109.txt\"),\n    (\"Apr 1939\", \"/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1939_06_page_117.txt\"),\n    (\"May 1939\", \"/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1939_07_page_109.txt\"),\n    (\"Jun 1939\", \"/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1939_08_page_117.txt\"),\n    (\"Jul 1939\", \"/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1939_09_page_66.txt\"),\n    (\"Aug 1939\", \"/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1939_10_page_68.txt\"),\n    (\"Sep 1939\", \"/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1939_11_page_70.txt\"),\n    (\"Oct 1939\", \"/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1939_12_page_72.txt\"),\n]:\n    with open(filepath, 'r') as f:\n        content = f.read()\n    \n    # Find all numbers in the Cases closed column for Internal Revenue\n    # Pattern: find the row with \"Internal Revenue\" or look for the 4th cell after \"Internal\" or \"Revenue\"\n    \n    # For the inventory table, find all rows that could be Internal Revenue\n    inv_match = re.search(r'INVENTORY OF CASES.*?DISPOSITIONS', content, re.DOTALL)\n    if inv_match:\n        inv_text = inv_match.group(0)\n        \n        # Find the Internal Revenue row(s)\n        # Case 1: Standalone row with \"Internal Revenue\"\n        match1 = re.search(r'Internal Revenue.*?<td>(\\d+)</td><td>(\\d+)</td><td>(\\d+)', inv_text)\n        # Case 2: Row with just \"Revenue....\"\n        match2 = re.search(r'Revenue.*?<td>(\\d+)</td><td>(\\d+)</td><td>(\\d+)', inv_text)\n        \n        cases = None\n        if match1:\n            # Third group is cases closed\n            cases = int(match1.group(3))\n        elif match2:\n            cases = int(match2.group(3))\n        \n        print(f\"{month}: {cases} cases\")\n    else:\n        print(f\"{month}: Could not find inventory table\")\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
